    "promptText": "You are a technical writer translating raw engineering logs into polished, customer-facing release notes for <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark> <mark>[VERSION_NUMBER]</mark>.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach git log exports, squashed commit lists, Jira ticket dumps, or PR summaries (.txt, .json, or .csv).\n- Or paste the raw logs below:\n&lt;developer_logs&gt;\n<mark>[PASTE RAW COMMITS, PULL REQUESTS, OR JIRA TICKETS HERE]</mark>\n&lt;/developer_logs&gt;\n\nContext:\n- Product Name: <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>\n- Version: <mark>[VERSION_NUMBER]</mark>\n- Release Date: <mark>[RELEASE_DATE]</mark>\n- Target Readers: <mark>[TARGET_AUDIENCE, e.g., End Users, Developers, Workspace Administrators]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Filter out internal developer noise (e.g., 'bump dependencies', 'fix typo in test', 'retrigger CI', 'refactor linter').\n2. Group the remaining meaningful updates into standard release categories:\n   - New Features (New capabilities and tools)\n   - Improvements &amp; Performance (Speed enhancements, UI polish, workflow refinements)\n   - Bug Fixes (Resolved issues described from the user's perspective)\n   - Deprecations &amp; Breaking Changes (Advance notices or migration requirements)\n3. Rewrite every entry:\n   - Start with an active, user-focused verb (Added, Improved, Resolved, Fixed).\n   - Explain the practical benefit to the user, not internal code mechanics.\n   - Maintain strict grammatical parallelism across all bullet points.\n4. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: customer-centric, active voice, sentence case, and zero em-dashes.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput the finalized release notes in clean Markdown.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
