    "id": "p17",
    "number": "17",
    "categoryId": "strategy",
    "categoryName": "Strategy & Planning",
    "title": "Localization Prep: Simplify for Translation",
    "useCase": "Optimize technical documentation for global translation and localization readiness",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "badgeColor": "#14b8a6",
    "promptText": "You are a Globalization and Localization (G11N/L10N) Technical Editor.\n\nPrepare the following technical documentation for translation into multiple global languages.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your draft documentation file (.md or .docx).\n- Or paste the text to localize here:\n<mark>[PASTE DRAFT DOCUMENTATION HERE]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;parameters&gt;\n- Target translation languages: <mark>[e.g., Japanese, German, Spanish, Simplified Chinese]</mark>\n- Standard: Global English / Simplified Technical English (STE) principles.\n&lt;/parameters&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Analyze and rewrite the text to remove localization blockers:\n   - Idioms &amp; Metaphors: Replace culturally specific idioms (e.g., 'kill two birds with one stone', 'under the hood') with literal, precise statements.\n   - Ambiguous Phrasing: Clarify polysemous words (e.g., 'since' when meaning 'because', 'once' when meaning 'after').\n   - Nominal Compounds: Break up long noun clusters (e.g., 'client configuration management service interface').\n   - Sentence Length: Reduce long compound sentences to 15-20 words maximum.\n   - Terminology: Ensure consistent noun usage (do not alternate between 'app', 'program', and 'tool').\n2. Provide:\n   - Localization Readiness Score (0-100).\n   - Localization Flags Table: Excerpt | Issue | Recommended Global English Replacement.\n   - Fully Revised Global English Version ready for translation engines and human translators.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput the findings table followed by the translated-ready document in Markdown.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are a Globalization and Localization (G11N/L10N) Technical Editor.\n\nPrepare the following technical documentation for translation into multiple global languages.\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your draft documentation file (.md or .docx).\n- Or paste the text to localize here:\n[PASTE DRAFT DOCUMENTATION HERE]\n</input_sources>\n\n<parameters>\n- Target translation languages: [e.g., Japanese, German, Spanish, Simplified Chinese]\n- Standard: Global English / Simplified Technical English (STE) principles.\n</parameters>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Analyze and rewrite the text to remove localization blockers:\n   - Idioms & Metaphors: Replace culturally specific idioms (e.g., 'kill two birds with one stone', 'under the hood') with literal, precise statements.\n   - Ambiguous Phrasing: Clarify polysemous words (e.g., 'since' when meaning 'because', 'once' when meaning 'after').\n   - Nominal Compounds: Break up long noun clusters (e.g., 'client configuration management service interface').\n   - Sentence Length: Reduce long compound sentences to 15-20 words maximum.\n   - Terminology: Ensure consistent noun usage (do not alternate between 'app', 'program', and 'tool').\n2. Provide:\n   - Localization Readiness Score (0-100).\n   - Localization Flags Table: Excerpt | Issue | Recommended Global English Replacement.\n   - Fully Revised Global English Version ready for translation engines and human translators.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput the findings table followed by the translated-ready document in Markdown.\n</output_format>",
    "proTip": "Attach drafts intended for international markets. The model eliminates confusing American idioms and complex noun strings.",
