    "id": "p18",
    "number": "18",
    "categoryId": "strategy",
    "categoryName": "Strategy & Planning",
    "title": "Build a Style Guide from Scratch",
    "useCase": "Build a comprehensive, customized documentation style guide from scratch",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "badgeColor": "#14b8a6",
    "promptText": "You are a Principal Documentation Architect creating an organization-wide documentation style guide.\n\nBuild a customized, production-ready Documentation Style Guide for <mark>[COMPANY OR PROJECT NAME]</mark>.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (optional):\n- Attach existing brand guidelines, sample good/bad docs, or engineering culture notes.\n- Or describe your documentation goals and toolchain here:\n<mark>[DESCRIBE COMPANY CULTURE, TOOLS, AND GOALS HERE]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;parameters&gt;\n- Company/Project: <mark>[COMPANY OR PROJECT NAME]</mark>\n- Toolchain: <mark>[e.g., Docs-as-Code (Git, Markdown, Hugo/Docusaurus), Notion, Confluence, Readme.com]</mark>\n- Audience: <mark>[e.g., Enterprise B2B developers, Internal engineers, Consumer end users]</mark>\n- Core voice attributes: <mark>[e.g., Authoritative yet approachable, concise, developer-first]</mark>\n&lt;/parameters&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\nDraft a comprehensive style guide covering:\n1. Voice and Tone: Core principles with 'Do This / Don't Do That' examples.\n2. Formatting &amp; Structure: Heading capitalization (sentence case), list conventions, code block formatting, and alert callout standards.\n3. Terminology &amp; Word List: Table of 10-15 preferred vs. discouraged industry terms.\n4. Grammar &amp; Mechanics: Numbers, dates, contractions, abbreviations, and punctuation rules.\n5. Code Samples &amp; APIs: Rules for placeholder brackets, comments, curl syntax, and language capitalization.\n6. Accessibility &amp; Inclusivity: Gender-neutral language, alt-text rules, and descriptive link text standards.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput as a modular, ready-to-publish Markdown document for your engineering wiki or repository.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are a Principal Documentation Architect creating an organization-wide documentation style guide.\n\nBuild a customized, production-ready Documentation Style Guide for [COMPANY OR PROJECT NAME].\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (optional):\n- Attach existing brand guidelines, sample good/bad docs, or engineering culture notes.\n- Or describe your documentation goals and toolchain here:\n[DESCRIBE COMPANY CULTURE, TOOLS, AND GOALS HERE]\n</input_sources>\n\n<parameters>\n- Company/Project: [COMPANY OR PROJECT NAME]\n- Toolchain: [e.g., Docs-as-Code (Git, Markdown, Hugo/Docusaurus), Notion, Confluence, Readme.com]\n- Audience: [e.g., Enterprise B2B developers, Internal engineers, Consumer end users]\n- Core voice attributes: [e.g., Authoritative yet approachable, concise, developer-first]\n</parameters>\n\n<instructions>\nDraft a comprehensive style guide covering:\n1. Voice and Tone: Core principles with 'Do This / Don't Do That' examples.\n2. Formatting & Structure: Heading capitalization (sentence case), list conventions, code block formatting, and alert callout standards.\n3. Terminology & Word List: Table of 10-15 preferred vs. discouraged industry terms.\n4. Grammar & Mechanics: Numbers, dates, contractions, abbreviations, and punctuation rules.\n5. Code Samples & APIs: Rules for placeholder brackets, comments, curl syntax, and language capitalization.\n6. Accessibility & Inclusivity: Gender-neutral language, alt-text rules, and descriptive link text standards.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput as a modular, ready-to-publish Markdown document for your engineering wiki or repository.\n</output_format>",
    "proTip": "Attach 2-3 examples of documentation your team loves. The AI will extract their voice and structure your custom style rules around them.",
