    "id": "p49",
    "number": "49",
    "categoryId": "apidocs",
    "categoryName": "Sample Product Documentation Prompts",
    "title": "Create a Documentation Taxonomy",
    "useCase": "Organize docs by tasks, audience, and page type with structured metadata tags",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "badgeColor": "#0d9488",
    "promptText": "You are a principal information architect and documentation taxonomist. Design a standardized, comprehensive documentation taxonomy and metadata schema for <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your content inventory, sitemap, or docs repo file list (.txt, .json, or .csv).\n- Or provide product context below:\nProduct Name: <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>\nProduct Domain: <mark>[PRODUCT_DOMAIN, e.g., Cloud Security, Fintech Payments, DevTools]</mark>\nUser Personas: <mark>[PERSONAS, e.g., Evaluator, Frontend Developer, Backend Developer, DevOps Admin]</mark>\nSupported Frameworks / Platforms: <mark>[PLATFORMS, e.g., React, Python, Go, Docker, Kubernetes]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Establish a multi-dimensional tagging and metadata classification schema:\n   - Dimension 1: Content Type (Tutorial, How-To Guide, Conceptual Overview, API Reference, Troubleshooting, Release Note).\n   - Dimension 2: Target Audience / Persona (Beginner, Intermediate, Advanced, SysAdmin, Architect).\n   - Dimension 3: Product Capability / Domain Area (e.g., Auth, Invoicing, Webhooks, Compliance).\n   - Dimension 4: Technical Stack / Ecosystem (e.g., JavaScript, Python, REST, GraphQL, CLI).\n2. Create standard Controlled Vocabulary rules: naming conventions (kebab-case vs. lowercase), forbidden synonyms, and tag hierarchies.\n3. Provide a sample Frontmatter Metadata Spec showing how contributors must tag markdown files in YAML.\n4. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: clear, structured, and no em-dashes.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput the complete taxonomy guide and sample frontmatter schema in Markdown.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are a principal information architect and documentation taxonomist. Design a standardized, comprehensive documentation taxonomy and metadata schema for [PRODUCT_NAME].\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your content inventory, sitemap, or docs repo file list (.txt, .json, or .csv).\n- Or provide product context below:\nProduct Name: [PRODUCT_NAME]\nProduct Domain: [PRODUCT_DOMAIN, e.g., Cloud Security, Fintech Payments, DevTools]\nUser Personas: [PERSONAS, e.g., Evaluator, Frontend Developer, Backend Developer, DevOps Admin]\nSupported Frameworks / Platforms: [PLATFORMS, e.g., React, Python, Go, Docker, Kubernetes]\n</input_sources>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Establish a multi-dimensional tagging and metadata classification schema:\n   - Dimension 1: Content Type (Tutorial, How-To Guide, Conceptual Overview, API Reference, Troubleshooting, Release Note).\n   - Dimension 2: Target Audience / Persona (Beginner, Intermediate, Advanced, SysAdmin, Architect).\n   - Dimension 3: Product Capability / Domain Area (e.g., Auth, Invoicing, Webhooks, Compliance).\n   - Dimension 4: Technical Stack / Ecosystem (e.g., JavaScript, Python, REST, GraphQL, CLI).\n2. Create standard Controlled Vocabulary rules: naming conventions (kebab-case vs. lowercase), forbidden synonyms, and tag hierarchies.\n3. Provide a sample Frontmatter Metadata Spec showing how contributors must tag markdown files in YAML.\n4. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: clear, structured, and no em-dashes.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput the complete taxonomy guide and sample frontmatter schema in Markdown.\n</output_format>",
    "proTip": "Attach your sitemap or file list. The prompt defines controlled vocabularies and YAML frontmatter schemas to supercharge docs site search.",
