    "id": "p27",
    "number": "27",
    "categoryId": "apidocs",
    "categoryName": "Sample Product Documentation Prompts",
    "title": "Glossary",
    "useCase": "Build a standardized alphabetical glossary of domain terminology and acronyms",
    "level": "Beginner",
    "badgeColor": "#0d9488",
    "promptText": "You are a lead technical writer and terminology manager. Build an authoritative, standardized glossary of terms and acronyms for <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your architectural spec, user manual, or list of domain terms (.txt, .md, or .docx).\n- Or provide context and raw term list below:\nProduct Name: <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>\nProduct Domain: <mark>[PRODUCT_DOMAIN, e.g., Cloud Kubernetes Orchestration, Healthcare Data Privacy, Payment Processing]</mark>\nTarget Readers: <mark>[TARGET_AUDIENCE, e.g., Technical onboarding users, API developers, System administrators]</mark>\nTerms, Acronyms, and Jargon to Include:\n<mark>[LIST RAW TERMS, ACRONYMS, OR JARGON HERE]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Organize the terms in strict alphabetical order (A-Z).\n2. For each term, provide:\n   - Preferred Term: Capitalized per industry standards.\n   - Definition: A concise 1 to 3 sentence explanation written in plain language.\n   - Context / Why It Matters: Brief note on how it relates to <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>.\n   - Related Terms: Cross-references to other glossary terms (See also: ...).\n   - Deprecated or Avoided Terms (if applicable): Note outdated synonyms teams should avoid.\n3. For acronyms: State the expanded form in parentheses immediately after the acronym.\n4. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: clear, concise, active voice, sentence case definitions, and no em-dashes.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput the alphabetical glossary in clean Markdown with jump anchors or letter headers (### A, ### B, etc.).\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are a lead technical writer and terminology manager. Build an authoritative, standardized glossary of terms and acronyms for [PRODUCT_NAME].\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your architectural spec, user manual, or list of domain terms (.txt, .md, or .docx).\n- Or provide context and raw term list below:\nProduct Name: [PRODUCT_NAME]\nProduct Domain: [PRODUCT_DOMAIN, e.g., Cloud Kubernetes Orchestration, Healthcare Data Privacy, Payment Processing]\nTarget Readers: [TARGET_AUDIENCE, e.g., Technical onboarding users, API developers, System administrators]\nTerms, Acronyms, and Jargon to Include:\n[LIST RAW TERMS, ACRONYMS, OR JARGON HERE]\n</input_sources>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Organize the terms in strict alphabetical order (A-Z).\n2. For each term, provide:\n   - Preferred Term: Capitalized per industry standards.\n   - Definition: A concise 1 to 3 sentence explanation written in plain language.\n   - Context / Why It Matters: Brief note on how it relates to [PRODUCT_NAME].\n   - Related Terms: Cross-references to other glossary terms (See also: ...).\n   - Deprecated or Avoided Terms (if applicable): Note outdated synonyms teams should avoid.\n3. For acronyms: State the expanded form in parentheses immediately after the acronym.\n4. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: clear, concise, active voice, sentence case definitions, and no em-dashes.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput the alphabetical glossary in clean Markdown with jump anchors or letter headers (### A, ### B, etc.).\n</output_format>",
