    "title": "GitHub README",
    "useCase": "Craft a professional open-source project overview with badges, install steps, and quickstarts",
    "level": "Beginner",
    "badgeColor": "#0d9488",
    "promptText": "You are a developer relations engineer and technical writer. Create a stellar, modern, and engaging GitHub README.md for <mark>[PROJECT_NAME]</mark>.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your repository package manifest (package.json, pyproject.toml, Cargo.toml, or go.mod).\n- Or describe your project below:\nProject Name: <mark>[PROJECT_NAME]</mark>\nOne-Line Catchphrase: <mark>[TAGLINE_OR_VALUE_PROPOSITION]</mark>\nRepository URL: <mark>[REPO_URL, e.g., https://github.com/org/repo]</mark>\nLicense: <mark>[LICENSE_TYPE, e.g., MIT, Apache 2.0]</mark>\nTech Stack &amp; Runtime: [TECH_STACK, e.g., TypeScript, Node 20+, React]\nCore Features: <mark>[LIST 3 TO 5 CORE FEATURES]</mark>\nInstallation Command: [INSTALL_COMMAND, e.g., npm install @org/pkg]\nMinimal Code Example:\n<mark>[PASTE MINIMAL USAGE CODE HERE]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Structure the README.md following open-source best practices:\n   - Header: Centered or clean left-aligned project title, tagline, and shields.io status badges (build, license, npm/pypi version, PRs welcome).\n   - Overview: A compelling 2-paragraph explanation of what problem the project solves and why it is unique.\n   - Key Features: Bulleted list with bold feature highlights.\n   - Prerequisites &amp; Installation: Explicit CLI commands across popular package managers.\n   - Quickstart: Copy-pasteable minimal working example that produces immediate output.\n   - Configuration / API Options: Clear table of environment variables or primary configuration flags.\n   - Contributing: Quick summary pointing to CONTRIBUTING.md, issue guidelines, and development commands.\n   - License &amp; Community: License info, Discord/Slack community link, and contributor acknowledgments.\n2. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, friendly developer tone, and clean Markdown formatting.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput the full README.md in raw GitHub Markdown.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are a developer relations engineer and technical writer. Create a stellar, modern, and engaging GitHub README.md for [PROJECT_NAME].\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your repository package manifest (package.json, pyproject.toml, Cargo.toml, or go.mod).\n- Or describe your project below:\nProject Name: [PROJECT_NAME]\nOne-Line Catchphrase: [TAGLINE_OR_VALUE_PROPOSITION]\nRepository URL: [REPO_URL, e.g., https://github.com/org/repo]\nLicense: [LICENSE_TYPE, e.g., MIT, Apache 2.0]\nTech Stack & Runtime: [TECH_STACK, e.g., TypeScript, Node 20+, React]\nCore Features: [LIST 3 TO 5 CORE FEATURES]\nInstallation Command: [INSTALL_COMMAND, e.g., npm install @org/pkg]\nMinimal Code Example:\n[PASTE MINIMAL USAGE CODE HERE]\n</input_sources>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Structure the README.md following open-source best practices:\n   - Header: Centered or clean left-aligned project title, tagline, and shields.io status badges (build, license, npm/pypi version, PRs welcome).\n   - Overview: A compelling 2-paragraph explanation of what problem the project solves and why it is unique.\n   - Key Features: Bulleted list with bold feature highlights.\n   - Prerequisites & Installation: Explicit CLI commands across popular package managers.\n   - Quickstart: Copy-pasteable minimal working example that produces immediate output.\n   - Configuration / API Options: Clear table of environment variables or primary configuration flags.\n   - Contributing: Quick summary pointing to CONTRIBUTING.md, issue guidelines, and development commands.\n   - License & Community: License info, Discord/Slack community link, and contributor acknowledgments.\n2. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, friendly developer tone, and clean Markdown formatting.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput the full README.md in raw GitHub Markdown.\n</output_format>",
    "proTip": "Attach your package.json, pyproject.toml, or Cargo.toml to generate accurate badges, installation commands, and configuration options.",
