    "categoryId": "apidocs",
    "categoryName": "Sample Product Documentation Prompts",
    "title": "FAQ Page",
    "useCase": "Address common customer questions with clear, direct, and actionable solutions",
    "level": "Beginner",
    "badgeColor": "#0d9488",
    "promptText": "You are a customer-centric technical writer and support documentation expert. Create a comprehensive, search-optimized FAQ page for <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach recent customer support tickets, community questions, or product documentation (.txt, .md, or .docx).\n- Or list the raw questions and topic areas below:\nProduct Name: <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>\nTarget Audience: <mark>[TARGET_AUDIENCE, e.g., New Users, Enterprise Admins, Integrating Developers]</mark>\nPrimary Categories: [CATEGORIES, e.g., Getting Started, Billing &amp; Limits, Security &amp; Compliance, API &amp; Webhooks]\nRaw Questions or Frequently Encountered Issues:\n<mark>[PASTE RAW QUESTIONS, SUPPORT TICKETS, OR FORUM THREADS HERE]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Curate and organize the questions under logical, category-based H2 headings.\n2. Refine question titles so they reflect natural search phrasing and user intent.\n3. Write direct, concise answers:\n   - Answer the question in the very first sentence (avoid starting with 'It depends' without immediate context).\n   - Provide concrete steps, configuration examples, or links to in-depth guides where appropriate.\n   - Highlight limitations, pricing triggers, or security implications upfront.\n4. Add a 'Still need help?' contact and support escalation callout at the end.\n5. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, friendly conversational tone, clear sentence case headings, and no em-dashes.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput the complete FAQ documentation page in Markdown.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are a customer-centric technical writer and support documentation expert. Create a comprehensive, search-optimized FAQ page for [PRODUCT_NAME].\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach recent customer support tickets, community questions, or product documentation (.txt, .md, or .docx).\n- Or list the raw questions and topic areas below:\nProduct Name: [PRODUCT_NAME]\nTarget Audience: [TARGET_AUDIENCE, e.g., New Users, Enterprise Admins, Integrating Developers]\nPrimary Categories: [CATEGORIES, e.g., Getting Started, Billing & Limits, Security & Compliance, API & Webhooks]\nRaw Questions or Frequently Encountered Issues:\n[PASTE RAW QUESTIONS, SUPPORT TICKETS, OR FORUM THREADS HERE]\n</input_sources>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Curate and organize the questions under logical, category-based H2 headings.\n2. Refine question titles so they reflect natural search phrasing and user intent.\n3. Write direct, concise answers:\n   - Answer the question in the very first sentence (avoid starting with 'It depends' without immediate context).\n   - Provide concrete steps, configuration examples, or links to in-depth guides where appropriate.\n   - Highlight limitations, pricing triggers, or security implications upfront.\n4. Add a 'Still need help?' contact and support escalation callout at the end.\n5. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, friendly conversational tone, clear sentence case headings, and no em-dashes.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput the complete FAQ documentation page in Markdown.\n</output_format>",
    "proTip": "Paste unedited customer support tickets or community threads. The model will extract the core questions and rewrite them into clear answers.",
