    "id": "p33",
    "number": "33",
    "categoryId": "apidocs",
    "categoryName": "Sample Product Documentation Prompts",
    "title": "Product Release Notes",
    "useCase": "Communicate newly shipped features, bug fixes, and upgrade notes to end users",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "badgeColor": "#0d9488",
    "promptText": "You are a lead technical writer and product communications specialist. Write engaging, professional Product Release Notes for <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark> <mark>[VERSION_NUMBER]</mark>.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your sprint summary, changelog diff, PR list, or Jira release export (.txt, .md, or .json).\n- Or provide release notes input below:\nProduct Name: <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>\nVersion: <mark>[VERSION_NUMBER]</mark>\nRelease Date: <mark>[RELEASE_DATE]</mark>\nTarget Audience: <mark>[AUDIENCE, e.g., End Users, Workspace Admins, Developers]</mark>\nRaw Feature Highlights:\n<mark>[LIST NEW FEATURES AND IMPROVEMENTS]</mark>\nRaw Bug Fixes:\n<mark>[LIST RESOLVED ISSUES]</mark>\nBreaking Changes / Deprecations (if any):\n<mark>[LIST BREAKING CHANGES OR MIGRATION STEPS]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Structure the release notes for maximum clarity and scannability:\n   - Header: Version, Release Date, and an enthusiastic 1-paragraph summary celebrating key upgrades.\n   - What Is New: Major features explained through user benefits rather than internal commit jargon. Use subheadings and short bullet points.\n   - Enhancements: Bulleted performance improvements, usability tweaks, and workflow optimizations.\n   - Bug Fixes: Parallel bullet points starting with past-tense or active verbs (e.g., 'Resolved an issue where...', 'Fixed a bug that caused...').\n   - Breaking Changes &amp; Migration Guide (if applicable): Clear warning callout with exact actions required to avoid service disruption.\n2. Apply the Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, customer-centric phrasing, parallel grammar structure, and zero em-dashes.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput the complete release notes in clean Markdown.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are a lead technical writer and product communications specialist. Write engaging, professional Product Release Notes for [PRODUCT_NAME] [VERSION_NUMBER].\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your sprint summary, changelog diff, PR list, or Jira release export (.txt, .md, or .json).\n- Or provide release notes input below:\nProduct Name: [PRODUCT_NAME]\nVersion: [VERSION_NUMBER]\nRelease Date: [RELEASE_DATE]\nTarget Audience: [AUDIENCE, e.g., End Users, Workspace Admins, Developers]\nRaw Feature Highlights:\n[LIST NEW FEATURES AND IMPROVEMENTS]\nRaw Bug Fixes:\n[LIST RESOLVED ISSUES]\nBreaking Changes / Deprecations (if any):\n[LIST BREAKING CHANGES OR MIGRATION STEPS]\n</input_sources>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Structure the release notes for maximum clarity and scannability:\n   - Header: Version, Release Date, and an enthusiastic 1-paragraph summary celebrating key upgrades.\n   - What Is New: Major features explained through user benefits rather than internal commit jargon. Use subheadings and short bullet points.\n   - Enhancements: Bulleted performance improvements, usability tweaks, and workflow optimizations.\n   - Bug Fixes: Parallel bullet points starting with past-tense or active verbs (e.g., 'Resolved an issue where...', 'Fixed a bug that caused...').\n   - Breaking Changes & Migration Guide (if applicable): Clear warning callout with exact actions required to avoid service disruption.\n2. Apply the Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, customer-centric phrasing, parallel grammar structure, and zero em-dashes.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput the complete release notes in clean Markdown.\n</output_format>",
