    "title": "Authentication Guide",
    "useCase": "Guide developers through obtaining API keys, OAuth tokens, and bearer credentials",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "badgeColor": "#0d9488",
    "promptText": "You are an API security specialist and technical writer. Write an exhaustive, developer-friendly Authentication Guide for <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your identity architecture spec, OAuth sequence diagrams, or security guidelines (.md, .pdf, or .json).\n- Or specify authentication details below:\nProduct Name: <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>\nAuth Mechanism: <mark>[AUTH_MECHANISM, e.g., API Key, OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow, JWT Bearer Tokens, mTLS]</mark>\nBase URL: <mark>[BASE_URL]</mark>\nToken Endpoint (if OAuth): <mark>[TOKEN_ENDPOINT]</mark>\nScope Model (if applicable): <mark>[SCOPES, e.g., read:users, write:payments]</mark>\nToken Expiration &amp; Refresh Flow: <mark>[REFRESH_DETAILS]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Construct the authentication guide covering:\n   - Overview: Explain the security model and supported authentication protocols.\n   - Generating Credentials: Step-by-step UI instructions to generate and safely store keys in the developer dashboard.\n   - Passing Credentials in Requests:\n     - Show the exact HTTP header format (e.g., Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;).\n     - Provide copy-pasteable curl, Node.js, and Python code examples.\n   - Token Lifecycle (if OAuth/JWT): Detail token expiration, token exchange requests, and refresh token rotation.\n   - Security Best Practices: Callout box on safeguarding secrets, avoiding client-side exposure, and rotating compromised keys.\n   - Authentication Errors: Table of 401 and 403 error payloads with explicit resolution instructions.\n2. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: direct second person ('you'), clear imperative instructions, and no em-dashes.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput the complete authentication guide in Markdown.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are an API security specialist and technical writer. Write an exhaustive, developer-friendly Authentication Guide for [PRODUCT_NAME].\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your identity architecture spec, OAuth sequence diagrams, or security guidelines (.md, .pdf, or .json).\n- Or specify authentication details below:\nProduct Name: [PRODUCT_NAME]\nAuth Mechanism: [AUTH_MECHANISM, e.g., API Key, OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow, JWT Bearer Tokens, mTLS]\nBase URL: [BASE_URL]\nToken Endpoint (if OAuth): [TOKEN_ENDPOINT]\nScope Model (if applicable): [SCOPES, e.g., read:users, write:payments]\nToken Expiration & Refresh Flow: [REFRESH_DETAILS]\n</input_sources>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Construct the authentication guide covering:\n   - Overview: Explain the security model and supported authentication protocols.\n   - Generating Credentials: Step-by-step UI instructions to generate and safely store keys in the developer dashboard.\n   - Passing Credentials in Requests:\n     - Show the exact HTTP header format (e.g., Authorization: Bearer <token>).\n     - Provide copy-pasteable curl, Node.js, and Python code examples.\n   - Token Lifecycle (if OAuth/JWT): Detail token expiration, token exchange requests, and refresh token rotation.\n   - Security Best Practices: Callout box on safeguarding secrets, avoiding client-side exposure, and rotating compromised keys.\n   - Authentication Errors: Table of 401 and 403 error payloads with explicit resolution instructions.\n2. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: direct second person ('you'), clear imperative instructions, and no em-dashes.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput the complete authentication guide in Markdown.\n</output_format>",
    "proTip": "Include curl and multi-language header examples showing how environment variables safely inject bearer tokens into API requests.",
