    "id": "p36",
    "number": "36",
    "categoryId": "apidocs",
    "categoryName": "Sample Product Documentation Prompts",
    "title": "Pagination Guide",
    "useCase": "Explain page-, offset-, and cursor-based navigation patterns for large API response lists",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "badgeColor": "#0d9488",
    "promptText": "You are an API architecture technical writer. Write an authoritative developer guide explaining pagination across list endpoints in <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach sample list endpoint responses or OpenAPI schemas showing pagination metadata.\n- Or provide pagination details below:\nProduct Name: <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>\nPagination Method: <mark>[METHOD, e.g., Cursor-based / Keyshot pagination, Offset-and-limit, Page-number-based]</mark>\nQuery Parameters: <mark>[PARAMETERS, e.g., limit (default 20, max 100), starting_after, ending_before, page]</mark>\nSample Paginated JSON Response:\n<mark>[PASTE SAMPLE PAGINATED RESPONSE HERE]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Structure the guide to help developers navigate paginated collections safely:\n   - Overview: Explain why pagination is enforced and which collection endpoints utilize it.\n   - Pagination Architecture: Explain the pagination strategy (e.g., cursor vs. offset) and why it guarantees consistency during concurrent writes.\n   - Query Parameters: Table with Parameter | Type | Default | Max | Description.\n   - Response Envelope Anatomy: Explain metadata fields (e.g., has_more, next_cursor, total_count, data array).\n   - Step-by-Step Flow: Walk through requesting the first page, extracting the cursor, and requesting subsequent pages.\n   - Code Sample: Provide a production-ready looping example in Python or JavaScript that fetches all pages automatically.\n   - Edge Cases &amp; Best Practices: Address empty results, reached-end conditions, and rate-limit conservation.\n2. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, developer-focused, and no em-dashes.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput the complete pagination guide in clean Markdown.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are an API architecture technical writer. Write an authoritative developer guide explaining pagination across list endpoints in [PRODUCT_NAME].\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach sample list endpoint responses or OpenAPI schemas showing pagination metadata.\n- Or provide pagination details below:\nProduct Name: [PRODUCT_NAME]\nPagination Method: [METHOD, e.g., Cursor-based / Keyshot pagination, Offset-and-limit, Page-number-based]\nQuery Parameters: [PARAMETERS, e.g., limit (default 20, max 100), starting_after, ending_before, page]\nSample Paginated JSON Response:\n[PASTE SAMPLE PAGINATED RESPONSE HERE]\n</input_sources>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Structure the guide to help developers navigate paginated collections safely:\n   - Overview: Explain why pagination is enforced and which collection endpoints utilize it.\n   - Pagination Architecture: Explain the pagination strategy (e.g., cursor vs. offset) and why it guarantees consistency during concurrent writes.\n   - Query Parameters: Table with Parameter | Type | Default | Max | Description.\n   - Response Envelope Anatomy: Explain metadata fields (e.g., has_more, next_cursor, total_count, data array).\n   - Step-by-Step Flow: Walk through requesting the first page, extracting the cursor, and requesting subsequent pages.\n   - Code Sample: Provide a production-ready looping example in Python or JavaScript that fetches all pages automatically.\n   - Edge Cases & Best Practices: Address empty results, reached-end conditions, and rate-limit conservation.\n2. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, developer-focused, and no em-dashes.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput the complete pagination guide in clean Markdown.\n</output_format>",
    "proTip": "Provide a working client-side looping code snippet (Python or JavaScript) showing developers how to automatically fetch all paginated records.",
    "tags": [
      "guide",
      "pagination"
    ],
    "domain": "tech",
    "domainName": "Technical Writing",
    "subcategoryId": "draft_content",
    "subcategoryName": "Draft & Content"
