    "categoryId": "apidocs",
    "categoryName": "Sample Product Documentation Prompts",
    "title": "Audit and Rewrite a Poor API Doc",
    "useCase": "Refactor incomplete or confusing API docs into clean, developer-friendly references",
    "level": "Senior",
    "badgeColor": "#0d9488",
    "promptText": "You are a principal technical writer and API documentation auditor. Audit and completely rewrite a poorly drafted API document.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach the existing poorly written documentation file or raw engineer notes (.txt, .md, or .docx).\n- Or paste the draft below:\n<mark>[PASTE POORLY WRITTEN API DOCUMENTATION HERE]</mark>\n\nProduct context:\n- Product Name: <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>\n- Target Developer Audience: <mark>[AUDIENCE, e.g., Third-party integrations, Internal backend teams]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Phase 1: The Documentation Audit:\n   - Provide a frank, structured critique highlighting: missing prerequisites, ambiguous parameter types, missing error states, unclear authentication, and formatting defects.\n2. Phase 2: The Complete Production Rewrite:\n   - Transform the content into an industry-leading API reference page.\n   - Include: HTTP method badge, full endpoint URL, authentication headers, complete parameters table (Name, Type, Required, Description), copy-pasteable curl example, annotated 200 OK JSON response, and standard error responses (400, 401, 404, 500).\n3. Phase 3: Editorial Summary:\n   - Summarize the specific technical changes made and how they reduce developer cognitive load.\n4. Apply the Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, sentence case, and zero em-dashes.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput the audit critique, followed by the complete rewritten documentation and editorial summary in Markdown.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are a principal technical writer and API documentation auditor. Audit and completely rewrite a poorly drafted API document.\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach the existing poorly written documentation file or raw engineer notes (.txt, .md, or .docx).\n- Or paste the draft below:\n[PASTE POORLY WRITTEN API DOCUMENTATION HERE]\n\nProduct context:\n- Product Name: [PRODUCT_NAME]\n- Target Developer Audience: [AUDIENCE, e.g., Third-party integrations, Internal backend teams]\n</input_sources>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Phase 1: The Documentation Audit:\n   - Provide a frank, structured critique highlighting: missing prerequisites, ambiguous parameter types, missing error states, unclear authentication, and formatting defects.\n2. Phase 2: The Complete Production Rewrite:\n   - Transform the content into an industry-leading API reference page.\n   - Include: HTTP method badge, full endpoint URL, authentication headers, complete parameters table (Name, Type, Required, Description), copy-pasteable curl example, annotated 200 OK JSON response, and standard error responses (400, 401, 404, 500).\n3. Phase 3: Editorial Summary:\n   - Summarize the specific technical changes made and how they reduce developer cognitive load.\n4. Apply the Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, sentence case, and zero em-dashes.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput the audit critique, followed by the complete rewritten documentation and editorial summary in Markdown.\n</output_format>",
    "proTip": "Paste unedited engineering notes or Slack discussions. The prompt systematically extracts missing parameter specs and formats them into standard docs.",
