    "title": "Convert Internal Wiki to External Guide",
    "useCase": "Sanitize internal engineering wikis into polished, customer-facing help center articles",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "badgeColor": "#0d9488",
    "promptText": "You are a principal technical editor and customer documentation lead. Audit, sanitize, and transform an internal engineering wiki article into a customer-facing help center article.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your internal wiki export from Confluence, Notion, or Google Docs (.md, .txt, or .docx).\n- Or paste the internal wiki text below:\n&lt;internal_wiki&gt;\n<mark>[PASTE INTERNAL WIKI / CONFLUENCE / NOTION CONTENT HERE]</mark>\n&lt;/internal_wiki&gt;\n\nProduct context:\n- External Product Name: <mark>[PRODUCT_NAME]</mark>\n- External Target Audience: <mark>[AUDIENCE, e.g., Enterprise Customers, Self-Serve Users]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Execute strict sanitization:\n   - Remove all confidential internal links, Jira ticket keys (e.g., ENG-4921), Slack channel mentions, and sprint references.\n   - Scrub staging URLs, internal VPN hostnames, test credentials, and employee names.\n   - Translate internal engineer shorthand or proprietary codenames into approved external product terminology.\n2. Restructure the article for an external customer:\n   - Provide a clear, professional H1 title.\n   - Add a customer-focused introductory summary explaining the feature's value.\n   - Organize procedural workflows into numbered steps with bold UI terms.\n   - Add troubleshooting tips for common user-facing errors.\n3. Provide a Sanitization Log table at the bottom summarizing every internal reference scrubbed.\n4. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, empathetic customer tone, sentence case, and zero em-dashes.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput the customer-ready article in Markdown, followed by the Sanitization Log table.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are a principal technical editor and customer documentation lead. Audit, sanitize, and transform an internal engineering wiki article into a customer-facing help center article.\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your internal wiki export from Confluence, Notion, or Google Docs (.md, .txt, or .docx).\n- Or paste the internal wiki text below:\n<internal_wiki>\n[PASTE INTERNAL WIKI / CONFLUENCE / NOTION CONTENT HERE]\n</internal_wiki>\n\nProduct context:\n- External Product Name: [PRODUCT_NAME]\n- External Target Audience: [AUDIENCE, e.g., Enterprise Customers, Self-Serve Users]\n</input_sources>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Execute strict sanitization:\n   - Remove all confidential internal links, Jira ticket keys (e.g., ENG-4921), Slack channel mentions, and sprint references.\n   - Scrub staging URLs, internal VPN hostnames, test credentials, and employee names.\n   - Translate internal engineer shorthand or proprietary codenames into approved external product terminology.\n2. Restructure the article for an external customer:\n   - Provide a clear, professional H1 title.\n   - Add a customer-focused introductory summary explaining the feature's value.\n   - Organize procedural workflows into numbered steps with bold UI terms.\n   - Add troubleshooting tips for common user-facing errors.\n3. Provide a Sanitization Log table at the bottom summarizing every internal reference scrubbed.\n4. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, empathetic customer tone, sentence case, and zero em-dashes.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput the customer-ready article in Markdown, followed by the Sanitization Log table.\n</output_format>",
    "proTip": "Attach Confluence or Notion exports. The prompt scrubs internal Jira keys, staging links, and employee handles while rewriting for customers.",
