    "id": "p61",
    "number": "61",
    "categoryId": "general",
    "categoryName": "General Writing",
    "title": "Create a TL;DR & Executive Summary",
    "useCase": "Condense long emails, reports, or meeting notes into 3 actionable bullets and next steps",
    "level": "Beginner",
    "badgeColor": "#3b82f6",
    "promptText": "You are an executive communications chief of staff. Review a lengthy document, report, or message thread and produce a high-impact, scannable Executive Summary and TL;DR.\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your report, RFC, meeting transcript, or email chain (.pdf, .docx, .md, or .txt).\n- Or paste your long document below:\n&lt;source_text&gt;\n<mark>[PASTE LENGTHY DOCUMENT, REPORT, TRANSCRIPT, OR THREAD HERE]</mark>\n&lt;/source_text&gt;\n\nContext:\n- Target Reader: <mark>[TARGET_AUDIENCE, e.g., VP of Product, Busy Engineering Director, All-Hands Team]</mark>\n- Decision Required: <mark>[IS_A_DECISION_NEEDED, e.g., Yes, budget approval needed / No, informational only]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Condense the material into a structured, executive-ready format:\n   - The TL;DR (30 seconds): Exactly 2 or 3 bullet points summarizing the core event, outcome, or thesis.\n   - Executive Context (1 paragraph): Why this matters right now and what problem it addresses.\n   - Key Decisions Made or Milestones Achieved: Bulleted highlights of finalized decisions.\n   - Pending Decisions / Blockers (if applicable): Clear callout of what needs immediate leadership attention.\n   - Action Items &amp; Owners: Table listing Action Item | Owner | Target Completion Date.\n2. Eliminate all conversational filler, redundant context, and backstory.\n3. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: concise, active voice, sentence case, and zero em-dashes.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput the Executive Summary and Action Items in clean Markdown.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are an executive communications chief of staff. Review a lengthy document, report, or message thread and produce a high-impact, scannable Executive Summary and TL;DR.\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your report, RFC, meeting transcript, or email chain (.pdf, .docx, .md, or .txt).\n- Or paste your long document below:\n<source_text>\n[PASTE LENGTHY DOCUMENT, REPORT, TRANSCRIPT, OR THREAD HERE]\n</source_text>\n\nContext:\n- Target Reader: [TARGET_AUDIENCE, e.g., VP of Product, Busy Engineering Director, All-Hands Team]\n- Decision Required: [IS_A_DECISION_NEEDED, e.g., Yes, budget approval needed / No, informational only]\n</input_sources>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Condense the material into a structured, executive-ready format:\n   - The TL;DR (30 seconds): Exactly 2 or 3 bullet points summarizing the core event, outcome, or thesis.\n   - Executive Context (1 paragraph): Why this matters right now and what problem it addresses.\n   - Key Decisions Made or Milestones Achieved: Bulleted highlights of finalized decisions.\n   - Pending Decisions / Blockers (if applicable): Clear callout of what needs immediate leadership attention.\n   - Action Items & Owners: Table listing Action Item | Owner | Target Completion Date.\n2. Eliminate all conversational filler, redundant context, and backstory.\n3. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: concise, active voice, sentence case, and zero em-dashes.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput the Executive Summary and Action Items in clean Markdown.\n</output_format>",
    "proTip": "Attach lengthy meeting transcripts, whitepapers, or email chains. The prompt distills pages of text into a 3-bullet executive briefing.",
