    "id": "p67",
    "number": "67",
    "categoryId": "general",
    "categoryName": "General Writing",
    "title": "Convert an Idea into an Engaging X/Twitter Thread",
    "useCase": "Turn an article, insight, or lesson into a 5-7 post thread with a killer hook and bookmark-worthy conclusion",
    "level": "Intermediate",
    "badgeColor": "#ec4899",
    "promptText": "You are a ghostwriter and social media strategist known for writing viral educational threads on X (Twitter).\n\n&lt;input_sources&gt;\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your blog post, essay, or research summary (.md, .txt, or .docx).\n- Or describe your concept below:\nCore Concept / Big Idea: <mark>[CORE_CONCEPT]</mark>\nTarget Audience: <mark>[AUDIENCE, e.g., Developers, technical writers, startup founders]</mark>\nKey Supporting Points: <mark>[LIST 3 TO 5 KEY LESSONS OR TACTICS]</mark>\nKey Takeaway / Final CTA: <mark>[DESIRED_ACTION, e.g., Repost the first tweet, bookmark the thread, follow for more]</mark>\n&lt;/input_sources&gt;\n\n&lt;instructions&gt;\n1. Craft a 5 to 7 post thread optimized for high retention and bookmark rates:\n   - Post 1 (The Hook): A 2-line hook that promises immediate, actionable value. Include a thread indicator (e.g., 'A quick breakdown:' or 'Here is how to do it: 🧵').\n   - Posts 2 through 5 (The Core Value):\n     - Each post must deliver a self-contained, actionable insight.\n     - Use generous whitespace and numbered bullet points.\n     - Keep every post strictly under 280 characters.\n   - Post 6 (The Synthesis / Summary): A quick recap distilling the entire thread into 3 memorable bullet points.\n   - Post 7 (The Bookmark &amp; Repost CTA): Ask readers to bookmark the post for future reference and repost the first tweet to share with peers.\n2. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, crisp phrasing, and zero em-dashes.\n&lt;/instructions&gt;\n\n&lt;output_format&gt;\nOutput each numbered tweet in the thread with character counts clearly indicated.\n&lt;/output_format&gt;",
    "copyPrompt": "You are a ghostwriter and social media strategist known for writing viral educational threads on X (Twitter).\n\n<input_sources>\nAttached documents (recommended):\n- Attach your blog post, essay, or research summary (.md, .txt, or .docx).\n- Or describe your concept below:\nCore Concept / Big Idea: [CORE_CONCEPT]\nTarget Audience: [AUDIENCE, e.g., Developers, technical writers, startup founders]\nKey Supporting Points: [LIST 3 TO 5 KEY LESSONS OR TACTICS]\nKey Takeaway / Final CTA: [DESIRED_ACTION, e.g., Repost the first tweet, bookmark the thread, follow for more]\n</input_sources>\n\n<instructions>\n1. Craft a 5 to 7 post thread optimized for high retention and bookmark rates:\n   - Post 1 (The Hook): A 2-line hook that promises immediate, actionable value. Include a thread indicator (e.g., 'A quick breakdown:' or 'Here is how to do it: 🧵').\n   - Posts 2 through 5 (The Core Value):\n     - Each post must deliver a self-contained, actionable insight.\n     - Use generous whitespace and numbered bullet points.\n     - Keep every post strictly under 280 characters.\n   - Post 6 (The Synthesis / Summary): A quick recap distilling the entire thread into 3 memorable bullet points.\n   - Post 7 (The Bookmark & Repost CTA): Ask readers to bookmark the post for future reference and repost the first tweet to share with peers.\n2. Adhere to Microsoft Writing Style Guide: active voice, crisp phrasing, and zero em-dashes.\n</instructions>\n\n<output_format>\nOutput each numbered tweet in the thread with character counts clearly indicated.\n</output_format>",
    "proTip": "Ensure Tweet 1 stops scrolling readers with an intriguing hook, and end with an explicit call to bookmark the thread for later reference.",
